body:
This risk assessment uses the best available qualitative and quantitative data from 2020 to 2025 inclusive. These five years represent the period since publication of the last NRA. The data available on VASPs is more comprehensive now than it has ever been in the past due to the submission of supervisory AML/CFT returns and continued industry engagement.

There are several sources, with the main ones being:

- data obtained from the FSA, including data collected from the annual (and ad-hoc) AML/CFT/CPF returns submitted by VASPs and other regulated sectors;

- data obtained from requests to industry solely for the purpose of this risk assessment;

- data from the Financial Intelligence Unit (FIU);

- data from the Proactive International Money Laundering Team (PIMLIT);

- data obtained from the Gambling Supervision Commission (GSC);

- data obtained from the Income Tax Division; and

- data from the International Cooperation and Asset Recovery Team (ICART) on Mutual Legal Assistance.

In addition, various externally published documents have been consulted, including several NRAs and Mutual Evaluation Reports from other countries, including the United Kingdom, given its close ties to the Island, to consider any relevant risks. The assessment has been prepared using informed judgement from the Island's key agencies, government, regulators and industry representatives. ■

The definition within the DBRO Act is fully in line with the FATF and defines VAs as a digital representation of value that can be digitally traded or transferred and can be used for payment or investment purposes. VAs do not include digital representations of fiat currencies, securities, and other financial assets. While VASPs are defined as any natural or legal person who by way of business conducts one or more of the following activities or operations for or on behalf of another natural or legal person -

- a) Exchange between virtual assets and fiat currencies;

- b) Exchange between one or more forms of virtual assets;

- c) Transfer of virtual assets;

- d) Safekeeping and/or administration of virtual assets or instruments enabling control over virtual assets; and

- e) Participation in and provision of financial services related to an issuer's offer and/or sale of a virtual asset.
Generally, VASPs in the IOM have demonstrated to have robust controls to combat ML, TF and PF, with a clear understanding of the risks. Deficiencies in the controls identified by the FSA as part of its ongoing supervision of the sector have been addressed or improved, and this is evident by the residual risk scores assigned by the FSA.

The IOM's DBRO Act, requires that an application to be:
To be registered as a Designated Business, the DBRO Act establishes that businesses must have, on a continuing basis, a minimum of two IOM resident directors and be managed and controlled in the Island. A "real presence test" is undertaken to assess that the VA activity will, in effect, be undertaken in or from the IOM, with key operational decisions taking place in the jurisdiction. The FSA has made it clear that the expected management and control requirements will not be met through the appointment of Directors provided by Corporate Service Providers.

- registered as a Designated Business includes, at a minimum, the following information:

- the applicant's name;

- a description of the designated business (Business Details Form);

- Business Risk Assessment;

- VASP questionnaire;

- any address from which the applicant carries on or intends to carry on such business; and

- details of any person who is a specified person in relation to the applicant (Specified Person Questionnaire).

The GSC undertakes different inspections based on operators' risk-ratings, including desk-based reviews, targeted inspections (desk-based + on-site reviews), and full inspections. All operators are subject to on-site full inspections, the frequency of which is determined on a risk based approach. Medium High and high-risk operators undergo full inspections every nine months. Findings from these inspections feed into the GSC's risk assessment of operators, determining the frequency of future inspections.
The GSC has been proactive in ensuring that relevant staff receive adequate training to fulfil their obligations. Since 2021, multiple internal and external training sessions have been organised and delivered to staff.
Although the GSC has issued extensive guidance relating to the risks involved with VA interactions, the authority is looking to enhance its general and software provider guides to update these with its expectations as well as to reflect emerging trends.

- Failure to apply relevant risk factors associated with VAs and their corresponding AML/CFT/CPF implications;

- The TRA did not consider risks related to VA acceptance;

- Use of anonymiser software in VA transactions was not recognised as a risk factor;

- Absence of blockchain analysis or monitoring to verify the source of wealth derived from VAs;

- No evidence of specific staff training on VA-related risks.
